// Original Stillwell editorial content. Clinical sources are linked in the page renderer.
export const serviceDetails={
 individual:{
  intro:['Individual therapy gives you time with a mental health professional to understand what is happening in your life and how you want to respond. You can bring a specific difficulty, a recurring pattern, or a feeling that is hard to name. You do not need a polished explanation before the first conversation.','Your work, relationships, family responsibilities, culture, and personal values can all be part of that conversation. The aim is to agree on a useful direction together, with space to revisit it as your circumstances change.'],
  focus:[['Stress that follows you home','Explore the demands on your attention, the expectations you carry, and where your own needs disappear in a busy week.'],['Patterns you want to understand','Look at familiar responses in relationships, self-criticism, avoidance, or difficulty setting boundaries without reducing your life to a label.'],['A change you did not choose','Make space for grief, a move, a relationship ending, or a shift in work or family life. You can talk about practical concerns as well as feelings.'],['Knowing yourself more clearly','Consider what matters to you, where you feel stuck, and what a more workable everyday life would look like in your own terms.']],
  session:['Begin with what feels most important today. Your therapist may ask about your history, current situation, previous support, and what you hope will be different. You can also ask how they work and what their qualifications mean.','Together, you can turn a broad wish such as “I want to feel less overwhelmed” into goals you can recognise in daily life. Depending on the approach, sessions may include reflection, learning skills, or trying agreed activities between meetings.','Review the fit as you go. Feeling heard, understanding the purpose of the work, and being able to raise concerns are useful things to discuss openly. You can ask about a different approach or provider if the fit does not feel right.'],
  faqs:[['Do I need a diagnosis to start?','No diagnosis is needed to make a general therapy enquiry. Explain the kind of support you are looking for. A qualified professional can discuss whether assessment or another service would be useful.'],['Will the therapist tell me what to do?','You can expect a discussion about options and goals. Ask how much the therapist uses structured exercises, reflection, or practical tasks. Your decisions and feedback belong in the process.'],['How many sessions will I need?','There is no fixed number that fits everyone. Ask about the proposed frequency, how progress will be reviewed, and what ending or pausing therapy might involve.'],['Can I talk in a language I am comfortable with?','Yes—include your preferred language in your enquiry. Confirm the individual provider’s fluency and availability before arranging a session.']],
  related:['generalized-anxiety-disorder','depression','insomnia']
 },
 'couples-therapy':{
  intro:['Couples therapy creates a structured setting to discuss the relationship you share: how you connect, where conversations become difficult, and what each of you needs. It can be a place to address an immediate concern or to understand patterns that have built up over time.','The purpose is not to appoint a winner in an argument. Both people need room to describe their experiences, hear the impact of their actions, and consider what they want to change. The goals might involve rebuilding closeness, navigating a transition, or making a thoughtful decision about the relationship.'],
  focus:[['Conversations that go in circles','Explore what happens before, during, and after familiar disagreements, including how each partner tries to be heard.'],['Trust and repair','Discuss hurt, expectations, and what rebuilding trust would require. A therapist can help you make the goals and limits of this work explicit.'],['Changes in shared life','Make room for the effects of parenting, caregiving, work pressure, distance, finances, or living with extended family.'],['Closeness and difference','Talk about affection, intimacy, identity, values, and the ways your needs may overlap or differ. You decide which topics to bring.']],
  session:['An introductory conversation usually explores why each person is seeking support and what each hopes to achieve. Ask how the therapist handles joint and individual conversations, confidentiality, and records before sharing sensitive information.','Sessions may focus on a recent interaction to understand the pattern beneath the disagreement. You may discuss ways to pause, listen, express a need, or return to a conversation more constructively, depending on the therapist’s approach.','Joint work is not appropriate in every situation. If there is fear, intimidation, coercion, or violence, seek individual professional advice about safety and suitable support rather than assuming a joint session is the right starting point.'],
  faqs:[['Do we have to be married?','You can enquire about support for a committed relationship without being married. Ask whether the provider works with your relationship structure and the concerns you want to discuss.'],['What if we have different goals?','Bring that difference into the first conversation. The therapist can help clarify whether there is a shared purpose for joint work or whether separate support would be more useful.'],['Can we join from different places?','Ask about the provider’s online arrangements, location requirements, and privacy expectations. Both partners need a setting where they can speak freely.'],['What if my partner does not want to attend?','Individual therapy can still be a place to explore your experience, boundaries, and decisions. It cannot provide an assessment of a partner who is not participating.']],
  related:['generalized-anxiety-disorder','depression']
 },
 lgbtqia:{
  intro:['Affirming therapy makes room for your identity without treating it as something to correct. You may want to talk about gender, sexuality, belonging, or coming out—or about work, sleep, relationships, grief, and the ordinary pressures of life. You set the focus.','A useful first conversation can include the language you use for yourself, what feels safe to share, and the parts of your background you want the therapist to understand. There is no requirement to disclose your identity to family, employers, or other people as a condition of asking for support.'],
  focus:[['Identity on your terms','Explore the words, experiences, and questions that feel meaningful to you. Your therapist should respect your name, pronouns, and pace.'],['Family and belonging','Discuss support, distance, expectations, or difficult conversations with family and community. Your safety and preferences matter when considering disclosure.'],['Relationships and connection','Bring concerns about dating, partnership, intimacy, friendship, or loneliness without having to fit a single model of a relationship.'],['Everyday pressures','Make space for the effects of exclusion or discrimination alongside the rest of your life. Ask about the provider’s experience with your particular concerns.']],
  session:['Before you begin, ask what an affirming approach looks like in practice. Relevant training, experience with LGBTQIA+ communities, and openness about the limits of a clinician’s expertise can help you assess fit.','You can agree how your name and contact details should be used, what appears in correspondence, and what confidentiality means in the service. Ask these questions before sharing information that could put your privacy at risk.','You do not have to make identity the subject of every session. Review whether the conversation is addressing your goals and whether you feel able to challenge an assumption or ask for a different direction.'],
  faqs:[['Do I need to be out to seek therapy?','No. You can enquire privately and discuss how to manage contact and confidentiality. Do not include sensitive identity details in the website’s test form.'],['Can I ask about a therapist’s experience?','Yes. Ask about relevant training, communities they have worked with, and how they respond when an experience is outside their expertise.'],['Will therapy try to change my identity?','An affirming therapeutic approach does not treat sexual orientation or gender identity as a defect to be corrected. You can raise concerns about any approach that feels disrespectful or coercive.'],['Can I talk about concerns unrelated to identity?','Absolutely. Your priorities might be career decisions, a difficult loss, stress, or a relationship. Identity can be part of the context without becoming the whole conversation.']],
  related:['social-anxiety-disorder','depression','generalized-anxiety-disorder']
 },
 women:{
  intro:['Therapy for women can make room for the person behind the roles: professional, partner, parent, daughter, friend, or caregiver. You may be carrying several responsibilities at once, facing a transition, or wanting space that is not organised around everyone else’s needs.','Women do not share one life story. Your age, identity, relationships, health, financial circumstances, and cultural context all influence what support should look like. You can define your own goals rather than being expected to follow a particular path.'],
  focus:[['Boundaries and responsibility','Explore the expectations you accept, the ones you want to question, and the difficulty of saying no when others depend on you.'],['Work, identity, and ambition','Talk about visibility, confidence, career changes, returning to work, or the tension between your own plans and outside expectations.'],['Pregnancy and becoming a parent','Make space for emotional changes, relationship shifts, uncertainty, or difficult experiences around pregnancy and the postpartum period. Medical concerns need coordinated healthcare.'],['Change across life stages','Discuss relationships, caregiving, loss, changing health, or a new sense of what you want from life. There is no single milestone you need to reach.']],
  session:['Start with your experience rather than a role you feel you should perform. It can be useful to describe a typical day, the moments that feel hardest, and where you have support already.','Ask about the therapist’s experience with the issues you bring. For pregnancy, postpartum changes, or concerns involving physical health, discuss how psychological and medical care could work together with your permission.','Your goals may involve understanding a pattern, making a decision, practising a boundary, or finding a way to ask for help. Review whether the work leaves room for both your relationships and your own needs.'],
  faqs:[['Is this only for pregnancy or motherhood?','No. Women’s therapy can address a broad range of experiences, and you do not have to be a parent or want children to seek support.'],['Can I request a woman therapist?','You can state that preference. Confirm provider availability and relevant experience before arranging a session.'],['Can therapy help with family expectations?','It can provide space to explore the meaning and impact of those expectations and consider choices that fit your circumstances. You do not need to reject or accept every expectation as a group.'],['What about postpartum mental health concerns?','Speak with a qualified healthcare professional about persistent distress or changes in functioning after childbirth. Psychological support may form part of coordinated care; urgent changes require timely medical assessment.']],
  related:['postpartum-depression','generalized-anxiety-disorder','depression']
 },
 military:{
  intro:['Service life can affect the whole household: time apart, changing routines, uncertainty about location, and the effort of reconnecting after an absence. Therapy can be a place to discuss these experiences without assuming that every military family faces the same concerns.','Stillwell’s information is written for people seeking care in India. This page is not a military health-benefit scheme, a fitness-for-duty assessment, or a promise about what a workplace will be told. Discuss the purpose of care and any relevant reporting requirements directly with the clinician.'],
  focus:[['Separation and reconnection','Talk about maintaining relationships during time apart and adjusting when routines change again.'],['Pressure and expectations','Explore the difference between what a role asks of you and what you can comfortably carry at home.'],['Family conversations','Consider how to talk with a partner, parent, or child about uncertainty and change in a way that fits your family.'],['Transitions in work and identity','Make space for relocation, changes in responsibilities, leaving service, or uncertainty about what comes next.']],
  session:['Ask whether the provider has experience with service communities and how they distinguish personal therapy from an occupational assessment. Be clear about the kind of help or documentation you are seeking.','You can start with the impact on everyday life without describing operational details or other people’s private information. Your therapist can explain what information is relevant and how sensitive material should be handled.','Include practical preferences such as language, location, privacy, and scheduling. If more specialised care is appropriate, discuss how a referral or coordination with an existing care team would work.'],
  faqs:[['Is this connected to TRICARE?','No. TRICARE is a separate US program. Stillwell does not claim TRICARE coverage or affiliation for its India-based services.'],['Can family members ask for support?','Yes, family members can enquire about their own support needs. Ask which provider and format are appropriate for the person attending.'],['Can you provide a service fitness certificate?','Do not assume a therapy appointment includes occupational certification. Explain the requirement and confirm whether an appropriately qualified professional offers that assessment.'],['Will my employer be informed?','Ask the clinician to explain confidentiality, its limits, and any specific reporting obligations before starting. This website cannot guarantee the rules for an individual employment situation.']],
  related:['post-traumatic-stress-disorder','insomnia','generalized-anxiety-disorder']
 },
 veterans:{
  intro:['Life after service can include changes in routine, identity, relationships, and the sense of belonging that comes from a shared role. Some veterans want to discuss experiences from service; others want help with the ordinary challenges of a new chapter. Both are valid starting points.','You do not have to describe yourself through a diagnosis or retell a difficult event to make an enquiry. Begin with what you would like support with now, and ask about a provider’s experience before deciding whether to work together.'],
  focus:[['A new daily rhythm','Explore the practical and emotional effects of moving into a different work pattern, retirement, or civilian routines.'],['Identity beyond a role','Talk about purpose, strengths, loss, and the parts of yourself you want to carry into what comes next.'],['Connection with other people','Make space for changes in family relationships, friendship, community, and how understood you feel.'],['Experiences that remain present','Ask about specialist assessment when sleep, memories, heightened alertness, or other concerns interfere with daily life.']],
  session:['A first conversation can focus on your present circumstances and the kind of help you want. A clinician should explain their approach and ask about existing medical or mental health care that may be relevant.','If you want to discuss trauma, ask about the provider’s training and the pace of assessment. You can talk about what would help you feel safe and what you are not ready to discuss.','Consider how support fits with physical health care, family responsibilities, and practical needs. Therapy may be one part of a wider plan, and some concerns require specialist or in-person assessment.'],
  faqs:[['Do all veterans need trauma therapy?','No. Service experience does not define a single set of needs. You can seek help for relationships, grief, career change, or another concern without assuming a trauma diagnosis.'],['Can a family member attend with me?','Discuss that preference with the clinician. Your permission, the purpose of the meeting, and confidentiality should be clear before another person joins.'],['Are veterans’ benefits included?','No specific entitlement or reimbursement is promised here. Confirm any benefits with the relevant organisation and ask the provider about accepted arrangements.'],['Can I continue with an existing care team?','Tell the clinician about current treatment. If coordination is useful, agree on consent and the information-sharing process before records are exchanged.']],
  related:['post-traumatic-stress-disorder','depression','insomnia']
 },
 seniors:{
  intro:['Later life can bring more than one kind of change at once: retirement, shifts in relationships, bereavement, caregiving, new health concerns, or simply a different sense of how you want to spend your time. Therapy offers a place to talk about your own experience of those changes.','Support should make room for your preferences and independence. You can ask about language, accessibility, appointment format, and whether you would like a trusted person involved. Age alone does not determine what you need or what a meaningful goal should be.'],
  focus:[['Loss and adjustment','Make space for grief, changes in health, or a familiar routine that no longer feels familiar.'],['Connection and belonging','Explore loneliness, family relationships, friendships, and opportunities for contact that feel comfortable and realistic.'],['Retirement and purpose','Talk about identity beyond work, changing responsibilities, and what you would like this chapter of life to contain.'],['Caregiving and being cared for','Discuss boundaries, dependence, frustration, and how to ask for help while retaining a voice in decisions.']],
  session:['Before arranging a meeting, ask about any hearing, vision, mobility, language, or technology needs. An online format may be convenient for some people; others may prefer or need an in-person assessment.','A first conversation can cover your current concerns, health context, medicines, and existing sources of support. Sudden confusion or abrupt changes in memory or functioning call for medical assessment rather than being assumed to be a psychological issue.','You decide whether to involve a family member or caregiver, subject to the clinician’s assessment and applicable consent requirements. Ask how private time with the clinician can be protected when someone helps with the appointment.'],
  faqs:[['Am I too old to start therapy?','You can enquire about support at any adult age. Discuss your goals, accessibility needs, and the provider’s experience with older adults.'],['Can someone help me use an online session?','Ask the provider how a trusted person can assist with setup while preserving your privacy. They should not automatically remain in the conversation.'],['Does Stillwell accept Medicare?','No Medicare coverage is claimed. Medicare is a US program, and this website focuses on India. Check local fees and any benefits separately.'],['What if memory changes are part of the concern?','Tell a qualified healthcare professional. Changes in memory or thinking can have different causes and may need a medical assessment alongside psychological support.']],
  related:['depression','insomnia','generalized-anxiety-disorder']
 },
 'unlimited-messaging-therapy':{
  intro:['Writing can be a useful way to capture a thought before it disappears or to reflect between scheduled conversations. In a therapist-led arrangement, messaging may provide another way to organise what you want to discuss and stay connected to agreed goals.','Asynchronous messaging means the clinician may read and respond later. It is different from a live chat or a video appointment. Stillwell’s website currently provides a test enquiry form, not a patient messaging inbox, so discuss the actual communication arrangements with your provider before starting.'],
  focus:[['Time to find your words','Consider whether writing helps you express something that feels difficult to say aloud or remember in a session.'],['A record for reflection','You might use an agreed messaging channel to note themes you want to revisit. Ask who can access or retain that record.'],['Continuity between meetings','Clarify whether messages are part of your treatment plan, what kinds of topics fit the format, and how they relate to scheduled sessions.'],['Clear response expectations','Agree on working days, response times, message limits, fees, and what happens when your clinician is unavailable.']],
  session:['Start by asking whether messaging is clinically suitable and actually offered by the chosen provider. “Unlimited” can describe a service plan’s sending allowance; it should never be assumed to mean continuous monitoring or immediate replies.','Use only the communication channel agreed with the provider. Ask how identity, privacy, records, notifications, and shared-device access are handled before sending anything personal.','Keep an agreed route for urgent concerns and technical problems. A delayed reply should not be the basis of an emergency plan. Immediate danger or a medical emergency needs local emergency services or an emergency department.'],
  faqs:[['Can I message a therapist through this website now?','No. The current form saves test contact enquiries for the administrator. It is not a clinical conversation and should not contain health details.'],['Is messaging the same as a live session?','No. A live session takes place at an agreed time; asynchronous messages may be read later. Ask how your provider combines these formats, if at all.'],['Does unlimited mean instant responses?','No such promise is made by Stillwell. Ask for written information about response hours, limits, availability, and any additional charges.'],['Can I use ordinary email or social media?','Do not assume those channels are approved for therapy. Ask your provider which channel is appropriate and what information can safely be shared there.']],
  related:['generalized-anxiety-disorder','social-anxiety-disorder']
 },
 teens:{
  intro:['Adolescence can involve changing friendships, pressure at school, family disagreements, questions about identity, and decisions about the future. Therapy can give a young person a structured space to talk about what matters to them, rather than only what adults think they should discuss.','A parent or legal guardian starts the enquiry on this website. Before care begins, the clinician needs to discuss age eligibility, consent, confidentiality, safety, and the appropriate involvement of caregivers. The enquiry form asks for the adult’s test contact details, not the teenager’s private history.'],
  focus:[['School and expectations','Explore workload, exams, transitions, motivation, and the difference between outside expectations and the young person’s own experience.'],['Friendships and belonging','Make room for conflict, exclusion, online experiences, and the changing importance of peer relationships.'],['Family communication','Consider how to listen, express needs, disagree, and ask for space while keeping care and safety in view.'],['Identity and change','Support a conversation about self-understanding, growing independence, relationships, and uncertainty without demanding immediate answers.']],
  session:['Ask about the provider’s experience with adolescents and the age groups they work with. A useful introduction gives the teenager a voice in their own goals and explains the role of the caregiver.','Confidentiality should be explained in language the young person and guardian understand. Discuss what may be shared, what requires action for safety, and how concerns will be communicated. Do not assume either complete secrecy or full disclosure of every conversation.','Planning may include the teenager, caregiver, and other professionals where appropriate and consented. Confirm the arrangements for online privacy, missed appointments, and urgent support before sessions begin.'],
  faqs:[['Can a teenager submit the form themselves?','The initial enquiry here must be made by a parent or legal guardian. A clinician will explain the service’s age and consent requirements before care starts.'],['Will parents hear everything discussed?','That depends on age, consent, applicable requirements, and safety considerations. Ask the clinician to explain the boundaries to everyone before the first session.'],['What if the teenager is reluctant?','Ask how the provider introduces therapy and includes the young person’s priorities. A conversation about their concerns may be more useful than presenting therapy as a punishment or a demand.'],['Is NYC Teenspace available through Stillwell?','No. NYC Teenspace is a separate US program. This website provides India-focused teen care information and does not claim that program’s eligibility or funding.']],
  related:['generalized-anxiety-disorder','social-anxiety-disorder','depression']
 }
};

export const conditionDetails={
 'generalized-anxiety-disorder':{overview:'Anxiety becomes a reason to seek assessment when worry is difficult to manage, persists, or interferes with daily life. Generalised anxiety can involve several areas of life rather than one specific situation. A clinician considers the overall pattern and other possible explanations.',signs:['Worry that is hard to set aside','Feeling restless or on edge','Difficulty concentrating or relaxing','Muscle tension or disrupted sleep'],approach:'Psychological treatments such as cognitive behavioural therapy may be considered, and medication is an option for some people. An assessment helps determine what is appropriate for the severity, health context, and preferences of the individual.',context:'Bring examples from different parts of your week: work, travel, family, quiet time, and sleep. Note the impact of worry as well as the subject of it. You can say “I spend a lot of time preparing for things that may not happen” without deciding on a diagnosis.',faq:[['Is everyday worry an anxiety disorder?','Not necessarily. Duration, distress, and impact on functioning matter. A clinician can explore the distinction rather than relying on one feeling or a checklist.'],['Should I choose therapy or psychiatry?','That depends on your circumstances. Ask about psychological support and whether a medical assessment would add useful information.'],['What if anxiety affects my body?','Physical symptoms deserve appropriate assessment. Tell a clinician about them instead of assuming that every symptom is caused by anxiety.']]},
 'social-anxiety-disorder':{overview:'Social anxiety can involve intense fear of scrutiny or embarrassment in situations with other people. Avoidance may bring short-term relief while making work, study, relationships, or ordinary activities harder over time. It is more than simply preferring time alone.',signs:['Fear of being judged in social situations','Avoiding meetings, calls, classes, or gatherings','Strong worry before an interaction','Replaying conversations afterwards'],approach:'A clinician may discuss cognitive behavioural approaches, including carefully planned practice with feared situations, and whether medication is appropriate. The pace and goals should be agreed rather than based on pressure to become a different personality.',context:'Think of one interaction you would like to handle more comfortably. Describe what you predict might happen, what you do to cope, and what you miss out on. This creates a practical starting point without requiring you to perform confidence in a first session.',faq:[['Is social anxiety the same as being introverted?','No. A preference for quieter settings is different from distressing fear and avoidance. Assessment considers the impact on your life.'],['Will I be pushed into difficult situations immediately?','Ask how the clinician plans and explains any practice. Goals, consent, and a manageable pace should be discussed.'],['Can online therapy be a starting point?','Ask about suitability. The format should support the work you need, including how learning will transfer to situations outside sessions.']]},
 depression:{overview:'Depression can affect how a person feels, thinks, and manages everyday life. Low mood or loss of interest may occur alongside changes in sleep, appetite, energy, or concentration. A professional assessment looks at the pattern over time and considers physical health and other conditions.',signs:['Persistent low or empty mood','Less interest in previously meaningful activities','Changes in sleep, appetite, or energy','Difficulty concentrating or managing everyday tasks'],approach:'Treatment may involve psychotherapy, medication, or a combination, depending on the person’s needs. A clinician can discuss available approaches, ongoing review, and whether more intensive support is needed.',context:'You can bring a short account of what has changed: a task that now feels harder, a routine you have stopped, or how your days differ from earlier months. If explaining everything feels exhausting, begin with one example and ask to take the conversation slowly.',faq:[['Do I have to feel sad all the time?','People describe depression differently. Loss of interest, irritability, or changes in functioning can also be relevant, so describe your experience in your own words.'],['Can I seek support while taking medication?','Tell the clinician about current care and medicines. Psychological and medical support may be coordinated with your permission.'],['When should I seek urgent help?','If there is immediate danger, an inability to stay safe, or a medical emergency, contact local emergency services or go to the nearest emergency department. Do not wait for an enquiry response.']]},
 'attention-deficit-hyperactivity-disorder':{overview:'ADHD involves a persistent pattern of inattention, hyperactivity, impulsivity, or a combination that affects functioning. Assessment considers developmental history and difficulties across settings. A short online quiz cannot establish the diagnosis or rule out other explanations.',signs:['Ongoing difficulty organising or completing tasks','Losing track of everyday items or commitments','Restlessness or difficulty waiting','Impulsive actions that cause recurring difficulties'],approach:'Care may include medication, psychological or behavioural approaches, and practical support for daily functioning. The plan depends on age, assessment findings, health history, and the settings in which difficulties occur.',context:'Bring concrete examples from school, work, home, or relationships, including what already helps. If you have relevant earlier records, ask how to share them securely. Do not upload school reports, medical files, or a child’s details through the test enquiry form.',faq:[['Can ADHD be assessed in adulthood?','Adults can seek an assessment. The clinician will explore current difficulties and earlier history rather than relying only on recent concentration problems.'],['Does difficulty focusing always mean ADHD?','No. Sleep, stress, mood, medical issues, and other factors can affect attention. Assessment helps distinguish between possible causes.'],['Does an enquiry lead to a prescription?','No. The form is not a diagnostic or prescribing service. A qualified clinician must assess the situation and discuss suitable options.']]},
 'bipolar-disorder':{overview:'Bipolar disorder involves episodes of changes in mood, energy, and activity that can include mania or hypomania and depression. It is not simply frequent everyday mood changes. Understanding the pattern over time is important for assessment and treatment planning.',signs:['Periods of unusually elevated or irritable mood','Marked changes in sleep needs and energy','Changes in activity, judgement, or impulsivity','Episodes of low mood or reduced functioning'],approach:'Treatment commonly involves medication and may include psychotherapy and practical support. Ongoing follow-up helps review mood changes, treatment response, and warning signs. A psychiatrist can discuss whether urgent or in-person assessment is needed.',context:'Prepare a timeline rather than a single snapshot of how you feel today. Include changes that other people noticed, past treatment, and periods when things were relatively stable. A trusted person may contribute information if you want and the clinician agrees.',faq:[['Can a mood-tracking app diagnose bipolar disorder?','No. Notes may support a conversation, but diagnosis needs a clinical assessment and an understanding of the longer-term pattern.'],['Why discuss sleep at an appointment?','Changes in sleep and energy can be important parts of the history. Describe what actually happened rather than only whether a night felt good or bad.'],['Can I stop medicine when I feel well?','Do not change prescribed treatment based on this page. Discuss benefits, side effects, and any wish to stop with your prescriber.']]},
 'obsessive-compulsive-disorder':{overview:'OCD can involve intrusive, unwanted thoughts or urges and repetitive actions or mental rituals. These experiences can take time and cause distress. Enjoying order or being careful does not by itself indicate OCD.',signs:['Unwanted recurring thoughts or images','Repeated checking, washing, counting, or mental rituals','A strong urge to seek certainty or reassurance','Time lost to routines that feel difficult to resist'],approach:'Cognitive behavioural therapy, particularly exposure and response prevention, is an established treatment approach. Medication may also be considered. Ask whether a provider has specific training and experience in treating OCD.',context:'You can describe the cycle without detailing every thought: what triggers discomfort, what you feel compelled to do, and how long relief lasts. Ask how the therapist would distinguish helpful support from repeated reassurance that becomes part of the pattern.',faq:[['Does an intrusive thought describe what I want?','An unwanted thought is not the same as an intention. A clinician can help you discuss its meaning and impact without relying on assumptions.'],['What is exposure and response prevention?','It is a structured therapy approach involving planned contact with triggers while learning not to perform the usual compulsive response. A trained clinician should explain and guide the process.'],['Can OCD involve rituals nobody sees?','Yes, compulsions can be mental as well as observable. Explain what happens internally, not only what another person might notice.']]},
 insomnia:{overview:'Insomnia involves recurring difficulty getting to sleep, staying asleep, or feeling adequately rested, with an effect on daytime life. Sleep difficulties may relate to routines, stress, health conditions, medicines, or other sleep disorders.',signs:['Difficulty falling asleep','Waking repeatedly or too early','Feeling tired or irritable during the day','Worry about another difficult night'],approach:'A clinician may review sleep patterns and contributing conditions and discuss cognitive behavioural therapy for insomnia. Medicines are not the only option and require individual assessment.',context:'A simple record of bedtimes, waking times, daytime rest, and how you feel can support an appointment. Include work schedules and caregiving responsibilities so the discussion reflects your real life. Mention snoring, breathing concerns, pain, or medicine changes rather than assuming they are unrelated.',faq:[['Is one bad night insomnia?','An occasional poor night does not establish a disorder. Describe how often the difficulty occurs and its daytime impact.'],['Should I ask for sleeping tablets first?','Ask for an assessment of the cause and options. Decisions about medicines, duration, and follow-up belong with the treating clinician.'],['What should I bring to the first conversation?','Your sleep pattern, relevant health history, current medicines, and the practical limits on your routine are useful starting points.']]},
 'post-traumatic-stress-disorder':{overview:'After a traumatic event, some people experience persistent symptoms that affect daily life. PTSD may include unwanted memories, avoidance, changes in mood or thinking, and feeling unusually alert. Not everyone who experiences trauma develops PTSD.',signs:['Unwanted memories or distressing dreams','Avoiding reminders of what happened','Feeling persistently on guard','Changes in mood, trust, or connection'],approach:'Trauma-focused psychotherapies and, for some people, medication can form part of treatment. A qualified clinician assesses symptoms, safety, current circumstances, and the need for specialist support.',context:'A first enquiry does not require a description of the traumatic event. You can begin by asking about training, privacy, pacing, and what an assessment involves. Discuss practical needs that help you feel present and safe in the appointment.',faq:[['Must I describe the event in the first meeting?','Ask the clinician what information is needed and why. You can discuss pace and boundaries instead of feeling obliged to provide a complete account immediately.'],['Is every trauma response PTSD?','No. Reactions vary, and different forms of support may be appropriate. Assessment considers the pattern, duration, and impact.'],['What if the unsafe situation is ongoing?','Tell a qualified professional about current safety concerns. Immediate danger needs local emergency help; the website form is not an urgent response service.']]},
 'postpartum-depression':{overview:'Perinatal depression can occur during pregnancy or after childbirth. Persistent low mood, anxiety, or difficulty functioning deserves professional attention and is not evidence of being a poor parent. A healthcare assessment can distinguish different causes and levels of need.',signs:['Persistent sadness, anxiety, or emptiness','Loss of interest or difficulty feeling connected','Changes in sleep or appetite beyond expected disruption','Difficulty managing daily life or caring for yourself'],approach:'Treatment may include psychotherapy, medication, or both, with attention to pregnancy, breastfeeding, physical health, and practical support. Decisions should be coordinated with qualified healthcare professionals.',context:'It can help to describe when changes began, what support is available, and which parts of the day feel hardest. You do not need to present a grateful or capable version of yourself at an appointment. Ask how mental health care can connect with your maternity or primary care team.',faq:[['Is this just the baby blues?','Short-lived emotional changes and persistent depression are different. If symptoms continue, worsen, or interfere with daily life, seek a professional assessment.'],['Can treatment be discussed during breastfeeding?','Yes. Tell the prescriber or clinician about feeding and other health considerations so benefits and risks can be reviewed individually.'],['Which changes need urgent assessment?','Severe confusion, hallucinations, unusual beliefs, or immediate safety concerns after childbirth need urgent medical assessment. Do not wait for a routine appointment or test-form reply.']]},
 'panic-disorder':{overview:'Panic disorder can involve recurrent unexpected panic attacks and ongoing fear of further attacks. Panic can feel intensely physical. Similar symptoms can also have medical causes, so new or concerning physical symptoms should not be self-labelled as anxiety.',signs:['Sudden episodes of intense fear','A racing heart, trembling, or breathlessness','Worry about having another attack','Avoiding places because escape feels difficult'],approach:'A clinician may discuss psychological treatment such as cognitive behavioural therapy and whether medication is appropriate. The assessment also considers physical health and other causes of symptoms.',context:'Describe what happens before, during, and after an episode, how often it occurs, and how it changes your activities. Bring information about relevant medical checks and medicines. Ask how the treatment plan will address both fear of attacks and the situations you have begun to avoid.',faq:[['Can I assume chest pain is a panic attack?','No. New, severe, or concerning chest pain or breathing difficulty requires appropriate medical assessment. Seek urgent help if it may be an emergency.'],['Does one panic attack mean panic disorder?','Not necessarily. A clinician looks at recurrence, ongoing worry, avoidance, and the wider context.'],['What is useful to discuss in therapy?','Explain how the fear affects your choices and daily life, not only the sensations during an attack. This can help clarify meaningful goals.']]},
 'gambling-disorder':{overview:'Gambling can become a concern when it causes harm to relationships, finances, work, or wellbeing, or feels difficult to control. Support can begin before every part of life is affected. A professional can assess the pattern and discuss specialist help.',signs:['Spending more money or time than intended','Trying to recover losses through further gambling','Hiding the extent of gambling','Difficulty cutting back despite negative consequences'],approach:'A care plan may involve specialist psychological support and practical safeguards. Financial or debt advice may also be relevant alongside mental health care. Family members affected by someone’s gambling may need support of their own.',context:'Prepare an honest outline of the impact rather than a perfect explanation of why it happened. Consider what access, situations, or feelings are linked to gambling and what support you would be willing to involve. Do not put account numbers, debt records, or financial documents into the test form.',faq:[['Do I need to reach a crisis before seeking help?','No. You can seek advice when gambling starts to concern you or someone close to you.'],['Can family members ask for their own support?','Yes. They can discuss boundaries, distress, and their own wellbeing without attempting to diagnose or treat another person.'],['Will therapy solve the financial consequences?','Therapy and practical financial advice address different needs. Ask about suitable support for each rather than assuming one service covers both.']]},
 schizophrenia:{overview:'Schizophrenia can involve changes in perception, thinking, motivation, and functioning. Symptoms and support needs differ between people. It is a serious condition that calls for qualified assessment and an ongoing, coordinated treatment plan.',signs:['Hearing or seeing things others do not','Strong beliefs that others do not share','Difficulty organising thoughts or communication','Changes in motivation, social connection, or everyday functioning'],approach:'Care commonly includes antipsychotic medication and psychosocial support. Depending on needs, this may involve family education, practical rehabilitation, and a multidisciplinary team. Online information or general counselling alone is not a substitute for appropriate psychiatric care.',context:'If you already have a care team, bring its contact arrangements and your current treatment information to the assessment. With permission, someone you trust may help describe recent changes. Ask who coordinates care and what to do if symptoms worsen between appointments.',faq:[['Is schizophrenia the same as a split personality?','No. Schizophrenia is not a description of multiple personalities. A clinician can explain the condition and address misconceptions.'],['Can support involve family?','Family involvement may be helpful when appropriate and consented. Discuss the person’s preferences, privacy, and the purpose of shared meetings.'],['When is an online enquiry not enough?','An acute change in perception, severe confusion, inability to care for basic needs, or immediate danger can require urgent in-person assessment. Use local emergency services when necessary.']]},
 'eating-disorders':{overview:'Eating disorders involve serious disturbances in eating-related behaviours and thoughts and can affect physical as well as mental health. They occur across body sizes and backgrounds. Appearance alone cannot show how unwell someone is.',signs:['Persistent distress around food, eating, or body image','Restrictive eating or episodes of loss of control','Compensatory behaviours after eating','Eating patterns that interfere with health or daily life'],approach:'Care may require medical assessment, psychological treatment, and nutritional expertise. The appropriate setting depends on physical health and other needs; a specialist team may be necessary.',context:'You can ask about a provider’s experience with eating disorders before sharing your history. An initial conversation should consider physical safety as well as distress and practical circumstances. Do not use the enquiry form for weight records, food logs, or medical test results.',faq:[['Can someone need help at any body size?','Yes. Body size alone does not determine whether an eating disorder or medical risk is present. Seek an appropriate assessment.'],['Is this a weight-loss service?','No. This page concerns mental health and eating-related difficulties, not dieting advice or a programme to change body size.'],['Is therapy alone always sufficient?','No. Some people need coordinated medical, psychological, and nutritional care. Fainting, severe weakness, dehydration, or immediate safety concerns require prompt medical attention.']]}
};

export const medicationDetails={
 abilify:{use:'Aripiprazole is an atypical antipsychotic used for conditions including schizophrenia and bipolar disorder. It may also be used alongside an antidepressant in selected cases. The reason for prescribing and the formulation should be explained by the treating clinician.',watch:'Discuss restlessness, sleep changes, movement symptoms, and metabolic monitoring. New intense urges, such as gambling or compulsive shopping, should be reported to the prescriber. Antipsychotics carry a serious warning for older adults with dementia-related psychosis.',question:'What changes in movement, appetite, restlessness, or impulse control should I report?',related:['schizophrenia','bipolar-disorder']},
 buspar:{use:'Buspirone is an anti-anxiety medicine. It is different from benzodiazepines and is not intended to be treated as an instant response to every anxious moment. A prescriber determines whether it fits the person’s symptoms and history.',watch:'Dizziness, nausea, headache, or nervousness can occur. Discuss other medicines, supplements, and liver or kidney problems. Some combinations, including monoamine oxidase inhibitors, can be unsafe; a pharmacist or prescriber should review interactions.',question:'How will we review anxiety over time and decide whether this treatment is useful?',related:['generalized-anxiety-disorder']},
 cymbalta:{use:'Duloxetine is a serotonin–norepinephrine reuptake inhibitor, or SNRI. It is used for depression and generalised anxiety, as well as certain pain conditions. Having more than one possible use does not mean it is suitable for every person with those concerns.',watch:'Nausea, dry mouth, sweating, or sleep changes can occur. Tell the prescriber about liver problems, alcohol use, blood pressure, and other medicines. Abrupt changes can cause discontinuation symptoms, so changes require a prescriber’s plan.',question:'How do my physical health, pain history, and other medicines affect the choice?',related:['depression','generalized-anxiety-disorder']},
 lexapro:{use:'Escitalopram is a selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor, or SSRI, used for depression and generalised anxiety. A prescriber considers the individual history, other treatment, and likely benefits and risks before recommending it.',watch:'Possible effects include nausea, sleep changes, sweating, and sexual side effects. Discuss other medicines and any relevant heart-rhythm history. Do not stop or change the medicine without advice; stopping suddenly can cause symptoms.',question:'Which effects should prompt a routine review and which need urgent advice?',related:['depression','generalized-anxiety-disorder']},
 lithium:{use:'Lithium is used in the treatment of bipolar disorder, including treatment and prevention of manic episodes. It requires careful monitoring because the amount in the body matters to both effectiveness and safety.',watch:'Blood tests and review of kidney and thyroid function may be needed. Illness, dehydration, salt changes, and interacting medicines can affect levels. Vomiting, worsening tremor, confusion, or poor coordination can be signs of toxicity and need urgent medical assessment.',question:'What is my blood-test schedule, and what should I do if I become unwell or dehydrated?',related:['bipolar-disorder']},
 propranolol:{use:'Propranolol is a beta blocker used for several cardiovascular and other medical conditions. It is also sometimes used for physical symptoms of anxiety. It does not replace an assessment of the underlying concern or suit everyone who feels anxious.',watch:'It can slow the heart rate and lower blood pressure. Asthma or breathing problems, diabetes, and other heart medicines are important to discuss. Dizziness or tiredness may occur. Changes or stopping require medical advice.',question:'How do my pulse, blood pressure, breathing history, and other medicines affect suitability?',related:['social-anxiety-disorder','generalized-anxiety-disorder']},
 prozac:{use:'Fluoxetine is an SSRI used for conditions including depression, OCD, panic disorder, and certain eating-related or premenstrual conditions. The relevant indication, formulation, and treatment goals should be clarified with the prescriber.',watch:'Nausea, anxiety, sleep changes, and sexual side effects may occur. Fluoxetine can remain in the body for a long time, which matters when switching or combining medicines. A prescriber must plan any changes and review interactions.',question:'If we change treatment later, how will the timing of a switch be managed?',related:['depression','obsessive-compulsive-disorder','panic-disorder']},
 trazodone:{use:'Trazodone is an antidepressant in the serotonin-modulator group. Clinicians sometimes use it for other reasons, including sleep concerns, but a sleep complaint alone does not establish that it is the appropriate medicine.',watch:'Drowsiness, dizziness, and a fall in blood pressure on standing can occur. Discuss alcohol, other sedating medicines, and heart conditions. A prolonged painful erection is a rare but urgent adverse effect requiring medical attention.',question:'How might drowsiness or dizziness affect driving, work, or other medicines I use?',related:['depression','insomnia']},
 zoloft:{use:'Sertraline is an SSRI used for depression and several anxiety-related conditions, including OCD, panic disorder, PTSD, and social anxiety. The appropriate use depends on an individual assessment and the prescribing context.',watch:'Nausea, diarrhoea, sleep changes, and sexual side effects may occur. Discuss interactions, including medicines that affect bleeding or serotonin. Changes should be planned with a prescriber rather than made abruptly.',question:'What should we track so that benefits and unwanted effects are both part of follow-up?',related:['depression','social-anxiety-disorder','obsessive-compulsive-disorder']}
};
